{:edb/db-before-t 1602 :edb/db-after-t 1603 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534915 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:52:56.765Z" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087387 1100 "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." 13194139534915 false] [17592186087387 1100 "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." 13194139534915 true] [17592186087387 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 2dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{2x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2x} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x} \\\\\ne^{2x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 2e^{2x} \\cdot y &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{2x}) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534915 false] [17592186087387 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087388 1100 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087388 1100 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0$." 13194139534915 false] [17592186087388 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int (3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (x^{3})} \\\\\n&= x^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{3}{x}y) &= x^{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2}} \\\\\nx^{3} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2} \\cdot y &= x\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nand carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3}) &= x\\cos x \\\\\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3})dx &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x\n$$\n\nand $v = \\sin x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x - \\int \\sin xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x + \\cos x + C \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x\\sin x+\\cos x}{x^3}+\\frac{C}{x^3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534915 false] [17592186087388 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087389 1100 "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087389 1100 "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1$" 13194139534915 false] [17592186087389 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 9x^2\n$$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 3x^{2}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}y) &= e^{x^{3}} \\cdot 9x^{2} \\\\\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\cdot y &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}}) &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$, making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}})dx &= \\int 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}dx \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= \\int 3e^{u}du \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{u} + C \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{x^{3}} + C \\\\\ny &= 3 + Ce^{-x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 3 + Ce^{-0^{3}} \\\\\n1 &= 3 + C \\\\\nC &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}\n$$" 13194139534915 false] [17592186087389 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186087816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319470 13194139534915 true] [17592186087816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319477 13194139534915 true] [17592186087816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319484 13194139534915 true] [17592186087816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319592 13194139534915 true] [17592186087816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319599 13194139534915 true] [17592186087817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319491 13194139534915 true] [17592186087817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319502 13194139534915 true] [17592186087817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319513 13194139534915 true] [17592186087817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319570 13194139534915 true] [17592186087817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319581 13194139534915 true] [17592186087818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319524 13194139534915 true] [17592186087818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319536 13194139534915 true] [17592186087818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319547 13194139534915 true] [17592186087818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319558 13194139534915 true] [17592186088087 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534915 true] [17592186088087 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x^{2}, y(1) = 2$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534915 false] [17592186088087 1145 "On the interval $x>0$ containing the initial point, divide by $x$ to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac2x y=4x$. An integrating factor is $I(x)=x^2$. Then $(x^2y)^{\\prime}=4x^3$, so $x^2y=x^4+C$ and $y=x^2+C/x^2$. Since $y(1)=2$, $C=1$. Therefore $y=x^2+\\frac1{x^2}$ for $x>0$." 13194139534915 false] [17592186088087 1145 "The first step is to write the equation in standard form,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot 4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={1}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319471 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1089 "q-17533" 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1100 "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1113 #uuid "4ed12697-47e6-57ab-8aa1-251a21737d10" 13194139534915 true] [17592186319470 1145 "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{